---
import { CONTACT_EMAIL } from '@/constants';
import DefaultLayout from '@/layouts/DefaultLayout.astro';

const lastUpdated = 'October 16, 2026';
const discordInvite = 'https://discord.gg/atlasos';

const linkedData = [
  {
    data: 'Level and XP',
    why: 'The leveling system and level roles. Kept after you leave the server so your level and roles are restored if you rejoin.',
    kept: 'Until you ask us to delete it.',
  },
  {
    data: "Moderation cases: your user ID, the moderator's user ID, the action (warning, timeout, kick, ban, unban, removal of a timeout), the reason given, and when it happened",
    why: 'Moderation history, appeals and keeping the server safe.',
    kept: 'As long as needed to keep the server safe (see Moderation records).',
  },
  {
    data: 'Per-moderator action counts (how many bans, kicks, timeouts and so on each moderator issued)',
    why: 'Staff workload and transparency statistics.',
    kept: 'Until the moderator asks us to delete them; then they are merged into an anonymous total.',
  },
  {
    data: 'Invite attribution: which invite code you joined with and who created it; invite codes and how often each was used',
    why: 'Shown in /userinfo; invite statistics.',
    kept: "Until you (or the invite's creator) ask us to delete it.",
  },
  {
    data: 'Message count per Wrapped season',
    why: 'Server Wrapped (yearly statistics such as the most active members). Only a number per season; no content.',
    kept: 'Until you ask us to delete it.',
  },
  {
    data: 'Starboard posts: the message ID, channel, author and star count of messages that reached the starboard',
    why: 'Avoiding duplicate starboard posts.',
    kept: 'Until you ask us to delete it.',
  },
  {
    data: 'Suggestions: the text you submit, its status, and which staff member accepted or denied it',
    why: 'The suggestions system.',
    kept: 'Until you ask us to delete it.',
  },
];
---

<DefaultLayout
  title="Discord Bot Privacy Policy | AtlasOS"
  description="Privacy policy for Atlas, the bot of the AtlasOS Discord server: what it collects, why, how long it is kept and how to have it deleted."
>
  <section class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-4xl px-4 pb-16">
    <h1 class="mb-2 text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-white md:text-5xl">Discord Bot Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p class="mb-8 text-sm text-white/50">Last updated: {lastUpdated}</p>

    <div class="space-y-8 leading-relaxed text-white/75">
      <div>
        <h2 class="mb-3 text-xl font-semibold text-white">Overview</h2>
        <p>
          Atlas is the Discord bot of the{' '}
          <a class="text-primary hover:underline" href={discordInvite} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            AtlasOS Discord server
          </a>
          . It is operated by the AtlasOS project. This policy explains what Atlas collects, why, where it is kept, for
          how long, and how to have it corrected or deleted. Atlas only works inside the AtlasOS server; if you are not
          a member of that server, Atlas does not collect anything about you. For the AtlasOS website, see our{' '}
          <a class="text-primary hover:underline" href="/privacy-policy/">
            main privacy policy
          </a>
          .
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="mb-3 text-xl font-semibold text-white">Summary</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-6">
          <li>
            Atlas stores your <strong class="text-white/90">Discord user ID</strong> next to a small amount of data
            about your activity in the server: your level, moderation cases, which invite you joined with, and a few
            counters.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white/90">Message content is not stored in Atlas's database.</strong> Atlas reads
            messages in memory to run its features and then discards them. Copies of deleted and edited messages, and of
            direct messages sent to Atlas, are posted to private staff channels in Discord.
          </li>
          <li>
            Message content is only sent outside Discord when you choose to use{' '}
            <strong class="text-white/90">Jarvis</strong> or <strong class="text-white/90">Translate</strong>, which are
            powered by Groq.
          </li>
          <li>We do not sell data, use it for advertising, or use it to train AI models.</li>
          <li>You can ask us to see, correct or delete your data at any time.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="mb-3 text-xl font-semibold text-white">Data Linked to Your User ID</h2>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-white/10">
          <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
            <thead class="bg-white/5 text-white/90">
              <tr>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Data</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Why</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Kept for</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/10">
              {linkedData.map((row) => (
                <tr class="align-top">
                  <td class="px-4 py-3">{row.data}</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3">{row.why}</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3">{row.kept}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="mb-3 text-xl font-semibold text-white">Aggregate Statistics</h2>
        <p>
          Atlas keeps counters that are not linked to any user ID, for example: how many current members have each
          onboarding status or profile badge, members by the year their Discord account was created, messages per
          monitored channel, how often configured keywords are mentioned per channel, new forum posts per tag, AutoMod
          blocks, server boosts, and the most used emojis and most reacted messages (by message link) of a Wrapped
          season. These are kept indefinitely.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="mb-3 text-xl font-semibold text-white">Processed in Memory and Not Stored</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-6">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white/90">Message content</strong> is read in memory to run auto-responses, count
            configured keywords, award XP, and check attachments and images (file type checks and reading the text in
            images to block scams). It is not written to Atlas's database. Images are downloaded only for that check and
            are not kept.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white/90">Presence</strong> (online status and the devices you are using) is used only
            to count how many members are online, idle or on do-not-disturb, and to show your platforms in /userinfo
            when someone runs it. It is not stored.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white/90">Usernames, display names and avatars</strong> are shown in replies and logs as
            Discord provides them; Atlas's database stores user IDs only.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white/90">Ko-fi supporters:</strong> when you support AtlasOS on Ko-fi, Ko-fi notifies
            Atlas and Atlas posts a thank-you message in the server with the amount and, only if you made your support
            public on Ko-fi, your Ko-fi name and message. Ko-fi's notification also includes other details, such as your
            email address; Atlas ignores them. None of this is stored in Atlas's database.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="mb-3 text-xl font-semibold text-white">Discord Log Channels</h2>
        <p class="mb-3">
          To moderate the server, Atlas copies some events into private staff-only channels in Discord: messages that
          are deleted or edited (including their content and attachments), direct messages you send to Atlas, moderation
          actions and their reasons, messages removed by the scam-image filter (who posted it, where, and the matched
          keyword), use of Atlas's commands, level-ups, and a record that a data deletion request was carried out (with
          the user ID it was for).
        </p>
        <p>
          These copies are Discord messages: they are stored by Discord, are visible only to AtlasOS staff, and stay
          there until staff delete them. They are not part of Atlas's database, so deleting your data with Atlas does
          not remove them automatically. You can ask us to remove log messages about you; we will do so unless we need
          them to handle a moderation case.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="mb-3 text-xl font-semibold text-white">Third Parties</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-6">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white/90">Discord.</strong> Atlas runs on Discord. Everything you post is also processed
            by Discord under{' '}
            <a
              class="text-primary hover:underline"
              href="https://discord.com/privacy"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >
              Discord's privacy policy
            </a>
            .
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white/90">Groq.</strong> When you use Jarvis (the AI assistant) or Translate, the
            message you send to Jarvis or ask Atlas to translate, with the context needed to answer it, is sent to{' '}
            <a class="text-primary hover:underline" href="https://groq.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
              Groq
            </a>{' '}
            to generate the reply. Groq processes it in the United States as our service provider and does not use it to
            train models. Groq does not keep it by default, but may log it for up to 30 days to troubleshoot errors or
            investigate abuse. Nothing is sent to Groq unless someone uses one of these features.
          </li>
          <li>We do not share data with anyone else, and we never sell it.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="mb-3 text-xl font-semibold text-white">Metrics</h2>
        <p>
          Atlas exports operational and community metrics (for example member counts, message counts per channel and
          per-moderator action counts) to a private Prometheus and Grafana instance operated by AtlasOS. Only AtlasOS
          staff can view them. Some series are labelled with Discord user IDs: moderators' IDs on moderation action
          counts and invite creators' IDs on invite usage. Metrics are kept for one year. When we delete your data, we
          also delete the series labelled with your user ID.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="mb-3 text-xl font-semibold text-white">Storage &amp; Security</h2>
        <p class="mb-3">
          Atlas's database and metrics are stored encrypted at rest on infrastructure operated by AtlasOS. Access is
          limited to the AtlasOS team members who run the bot. Before each database upgrade Atlas makes a backup copy of
          its database; the three most recent backups are kept and older ones are deleted automatically, so data you had
          deleted may remain in a backup until it is replaced.
        </p>
        <p>
          Application logs (errors and operational events) contain Discord IDs but never message content. They are kept
          for troubleshooting for no longer than 30 days.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="mb-3 text-xl font-semibold text-white">Moderation Records</h2>
        <p>
          If you ask us to delete your data, we delete your moderation cases as well, unless keeping them is necessary
          to protect the server, for example to prevent someone who was banned from returning under a new account or to
          handle an ongoing report. If we keep moderation records, we tell you which records we kept and why. Where you
          acted as a moderator, your user ID is replaced with "Deleted" so the cases of other members remain intact.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="mb-3 text-xl font-semibold text-white">Your Choices &amp; Rights</h2>
        <p class="mb-3">
          You can ask us to see what Atlas stores about you, correct something that is wrong (for example a moderation
          case reason), delete your data, or stop using your data for a specific purpose. Email{' '}
          <a class="text-primary hover:underline" href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>
            {CONTACT_EMAIL}
          </a>{' '}
          or open a support ticket in the{' '}
          <a class="text-primary hover:underline" href={discordInvite} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            AtlasOS Discord server
          </a>
          . We may ask you to confirm the request from your Discord account so we know it is yours. We respond within 30
          days.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-3">
          When we delete your data, Atlas removes your level, Wrapped counts, invite attribution, starboard entries and
          suggestions; your user ID is replaced with "Deleted" where you created invites or invited other members; and
          your metric series are deleted. If you stay in or rejoin the server, Atlas starts collecting the data
          described above again as you use it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Leaving the server does not delete your data automatically: your level is kept so it can be restored if you
          come back. Ask us if you want it deleted. Atlas is part of a Discord server, so it is subject to Discord's
          minimum age requirements; we do not knowingly collect data about anyone below them.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="mb-3 text-xl font-semibold text-white">Changes to This Policy</h2>
        <p>
          We will update this policy when Atlas starts collecting something new or uses data differently. The date at
          the top always shows the latest version, and significant changes are announced in the AtlasOS Discord server.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</DefaultLayout>
